<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Cumania</h1><p class="st-meta">1060 CE – 1241 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The vast steppe confederation of the Cumans or Polovtsians, partners and enemies of Rus', Hungary and Byzantium until the Mongols swept it away.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, called Polovtsians by the Rus' and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> in eastern sources, were Turkic nomads who moved west from the steppes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> in the 11th century. Around 1060 they displaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and in 1068 they defeated the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-alta-river-1068">Alta</a> river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Cumania was never a single state but a shifting union of tribes and clans under their own khans, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniak">Boniak</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharukan">Sharukan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konchak">Konchak</a>. They herded horses and sheep, traded through the Crimean ports and set up stone statues of ancestors on the steppe. Rus' princes fought them but also married their daughters and hired them in civil wars.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a> and Rus' were defeated together by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-kalka-river">Kalka</a> in 1223, and the Mongol campaign of 1237 to 1241 conquered the steppe. Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koten">Kotyan</a> led thousands into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kipchaks">Kipchaks</a> were sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where they became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a>. Their language became the common speech of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and is recorded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-cumanicus">Codex Cumanicus</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cumania.jpg" alt="Cumania" width="320" height="141"><figcaption>Image: Cumanian · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cumania_(1200)_eng.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1060 CE – 1241 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sharukan, Konchak, Kotyan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cuman</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>, <a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cuman-Kipchak confederation, Desht-i Kipchak, Polovtsians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1150&amp;focus=cumania" aria-label="Cumania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5424 454 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cumania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6487 875h0M6352 637h0M7559 780h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5744 710h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5744 710h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ukraine. <a href="/atlas?year=1150&amp;focus=cumania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
